# 0016. A git worktree per read-only agent

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-25

[0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr) decided (owner, 2026-11-24) that non-writers get their
own git worktree, so whatever escapes their read-only flags (a project hook, a detached
process) writes there and never into the writer's files. This ADR fixes the layout, how a
worktree is kept in step with the writer's checkout, and cleanup.

## Technical details
- **Who runs where**: the `writer` always runs in the checkout, as today: its writable
  turns with the lock, its read-only turns (broadcasts, `ask`) with the read-only flags.
  Moving those into a worktree would split its CLI session in two (see "stable path"
  below), and the writer is the one agent already trusted with the checkout. Every turn of
  every other CLI agent runs in that agent's worktree. Free models ([0005](0005-openai-compatible-models.adr))
  have no tools and need none. Outside a git checkout there is nothing to mirror: flags
  only, as today.
- **Layout**: `<data_root>/worktrees/<checkout-id>/<participant-id>/`, where
  `<checkout-id>` is the 16-hex FNV-1a of the checkout's absolute git root (the same hash
  family as identities, [0011](0011-hot-model-registry.adr)). Outside the checkout, so the
  writer's `git status`, file watchers and build tools never see it. Folder 0700.
  Created with `git -C <checkout> worktree add --detach <path> HEAD`.
- **A stable path per agent** matters: Claude Code files its sessions by working
  directory, so `--resume` only finds a session started in the same folder. One fixed path
  per (checkout, agent) keeps resume working. Sessions started in the checkout before this
  ADR cannot resume in the worktree: the first read-only turn after the upgrade starts a
  new CLI session and the chat says so once.
- **Sync before every read-only turn** (the agent must review the writer's current,
  uncommitted work, not just `HEAD`):
  1. `git -C <wt> checkout --detach --force <HEAD of checkout>` and
     `git -C <wt> clean -fd` (ignored files are left alone, so `target/` or
     `node_modules/` built there survive);
  2. `git -C <checkout> diff HEAD --binary` applied with `git -C <wt> apply --binary`;
  3. untracked, not-ignored files (`git ls-files -o --exclude-standard`) copied over.
  Done by the loop as one effect before the turn's spawn; a sync failure fails that turn
  with the git error (fail closed: no silent fallback to the checkout). Typical cost is
  tens of milliseconds; a large untracked tree is the slow case.
- **Not synced**: ignored files (build output, `.env`), so an agent that needs them builds
  in its worktree; and the worktree's changes never flow back. Read-only means the
  results come back as text only.
- **What the agent is told**: the turn's preamble says "you are in a read-only mirror of
  `<checkout>` at `<wt>`; give paths relative to the repo root", so replies don't point
  the user at worktree paths.
- **Cleanup**: worktrees are reused across turns and runs. `/model disable <id>` keeps it
  (history and sessions stay too, as 0011 says). Startup runs `git worktree prune` for
  each checkout seen, and removes `<data_root>/worktrees/<checkout-id>/` whose checkout no
  longer exists. A new `/worktrees` command lists them with sizes and
  `/worktrees clean [id]` removes them (`git worktree remove --force`).
- **The writer lock is unchanged** ([0012](0012-checkout-writer-lock.adr)): it guards the
  checkout; worktrees need no lock because only their own agent uses them, one turn at a
  time.
- Consequences: the single-writer rule becomes a filesystem fact, not only CLI flags; it
  is also the base for giving agents writable turns in their own worktree later
  ([0017](0017-writer-edits-from-any-message.adr)). Costs: disk (one checkout copy per agent, plus its build
  output), a sync step before each turn, and agents can't use ignored local files.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: `git worktree` (git ≥ 2.17 for `worktree remove`), `git diff --binary`, `git apply`
- Alternatives:
  - Worktree inside `<git dir>/roundtable/`: hidden, but tools that walk `.git` or back it
    up pick it up; the data folder is ours.
  - Fresh worktree per turn: no stale files, but breaks CLI resume (new path each time) and
    rebuilds from scratch every turn.
  - Copy-on-write clones (APFS `clonefile`, `cp --reflink`): fast, but platform-specific
    and not git-aware.
  - OS sandboxing per turn (`sandbox-exec`, Landlock): rejected in 0004 as heavy and
    platform-specific.
